1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,014
Incident response often stalls outside the system.

2
00:00:03,134 --> 00:00:12,178
An operator opens a vendor ticket, waits on hold, repeats context, and then copies unstructured notes back into the incident channel.

3
00:00:12,298 --> 00:00:16,604
IncidentBridge turns that delay into one controlled, authorized phone workflow.

4
00:00:16,724 --> 00:00:21,461
The key design choice is simple: calls become evidence, never authority.

5
00:00:21,581 --> 00:00:27,179
IncidentBridge can gather operational facts, but it can never declare the service recovered.

6
00:00:27,299 --> 00:00:35,912
Before any call, the request is validated for bounded incident identifiers, UTC timestamps, an authorized business recipient, and secret-like content.

7
00:00:36,032 --> 00:00:37,754
Preview is the default.

8
00:00:37,874 --> 00:00:46,917
It masks the recipient and shows the exact CALL-E task, result schema, idempotency key, and decision boundary without creating a call.

9
00:00:47,037 --> 00:00:57,803
Live execution requires four independent gates: explicit execute intent, confirmation that the recipient is authorized, an exact E.164 allowlist match, and a server-side API credential.

10
00:00:57,923 --> 00:01:01,368
Before dispatch, IncidentBridge writes a durable SQLite reservation.

11
00:01:01,488 --> 00:01:08,809
If the provider result is ambiguous, the reservation is locked for reconciliation instead of automatically dialing again.

12
00:01:08,929 --> 00:01:11,943
This evidence console exposes all three paths.

13
00:01:12,063 --> 00:01:15,508
The first is a real CALL-E provider run.

14
00:01:15,628 --> 00:01:21,657
The authorized number was unavailable, so CALL-E returned task completed false after eight seconds.

15
00:01:21,777 --> 00:01:28,237
IncidentBridge routed it to needs human, kept incident closed false, and made zero automatic retries.

16
00:01:28,357 --> 00:01:31,371
The second path is the safe preview.

17
00:01:31,491 --> 00:01:34,506
No call exists, and recovery remains human-owned.

18
00:01:34,626 --> 00:01:47,975
The third is a deterministic acknowledged simulation: a vendor ticket and status are captured, but even then incident closed remains false until local health checks and the incident commander verify recovery.

19
00:01:48,095 --> 00:01:53,693
The implementation uses the published CALL-E Python SDK at runtime, not a placeholder.

20
00:01:53,813 --> 00:01:58,120
Twenty-one tests pass with ninety-two point three seven percent coverage in GitHub Actions.

21
00:01:58,240 --> 00:02:09,436
The integration suite sends the real SDK through a loopback HTTP server and verifies the create-call request, bearer authentication, idempotency header, strict schema, and status polling.

22
00:02:09,556 --> 00:02:15,154
IncidentBridge is open source, MIT licensed, and contributed to CALL-E's official phone-agent repository.

23
00:02:15,274 --> 00:02:25,609
It gives operators a reusable pattern for real-world phone automation with consent, exact authorization, duplicate prevention, structured evidence, and a hard human decision boundary.